n gives the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d) bitset words per clique for degeneracy d.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"AllCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/allcliques","title":"AllCliques","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"AllCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/AllCliques"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/OnionLayers":{"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/OnionLayers","title":"OnionLayers","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"OnionLayers","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The onion decomposition of an undirected graph’s simple graph: each vertex’s layer, a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"refinement of the core decomposition in which every layer lies inside one k-shell. Layers are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"numbered from 1; isolated vertices, when there are any, are layer 1 alone."}],"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"OnionLayers","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/onionlayers"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/GraphProtocols":{"type":"topic","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/graphprotocols","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/GraphProtocols","title":"GraphProtocols","role":"collection"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques":{"type":"topic","abstract":[],"role":"collection","url":"\/documentation\/cliques","title":"Cliques","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/ClusteringCoefficients":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/ClusteringCoefficients","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Triangles and clustering for every vertex of an undirected graph’s simple graph, from one"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pass."}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/clusteringcoefficients","title":"ClusteringCoefficients","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ClusteringCoefficients","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ClusteringCoefficients","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/TrussNumbers":{"abstract":[{"text":"The truss decomposition of an undirected graph (Cohen; Wang–Cheng), over its simple graph. An","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edge’s truss number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every edge is in at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least k − 2 triangles: 2 for an edge in no triangle, k for an edge of a k-clique. Parallel","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"edges share their simple edge’s number, and a self-loop has none.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TrussNumbers","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"TrussNumbers"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/trussnumbers","title":"TrussNumbers","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"TrussNumbers"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/MaximalCliques":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every maximal clique of an undirected graph’s simple graph once, lazily, each in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order (by vertex index): an isolated vertex, or one whose only edges are loops, is a clique of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one. The order is Eppstein–Löffler–Strash’s: by the cliques’ first vertex in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"coreNumbers().degeneracyOrdering"},{"type":"text","text":", then as Bron–Kerbosch with Tomita’s pivot (ties to the least"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"index) and branches in ascending index meets them. The sequence holds the graph, so iterating"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"again gives the same cliques. O(n + m) before the first clique, O(d·n·3^(d\/3)) in all for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"degeneracy d."}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/maximalcliques","title":"MaximalCliques","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MaximalCliques","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/MaximalCliques"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/BronKerboschEvent":{"type":"topic","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvent","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"What the Bron–Kerbosch algorithm with pivoting reports, in order, as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maximalCliques()"},{"type":"text","text":" runs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"it."}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/bronkerboschevent","title":"BronKerboschEvent","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"BronKerboschEvent","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BronKerboschEvent","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/TreeDecomposition":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/TreeDecomposition","abstract":[{"text":"A tree decomposition of a graph: bags of vertices, joined by edges into a tree, such that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"every vertex is in a bag, both ends of every edge are together in a bag, and the bags holding","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"any one vertex form a connected subtree.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"TreeDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/treedecomposition","title":"TreeDecomposition","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"TreeDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/BronKerboschEvents":{"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The Bron–Kerbosch algorithm with pivoting as a sequence of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"BronKerboschEvent"},{"type":"text","text":"s: the search of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maximalCliques()"},{"text":", one clique at a time. Pulling an event past the last one kept runs the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"search to its next maximal clique and keeps the events on the way.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BronKerboschEvents","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/bronkerboschevents","title":"BronKerboschEvents","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"BronKerboschEvents","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/BronKerboschEvents"},"doc://Cliques/documentation/Cliques/CoreNumbers":{"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Cliques\/documentation\/Cliques\/CoreNumbers","title":"CoreNumbers","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"The core decomposition of an undirected graph (Batagelj–Zaversnik), over its simple graph. A","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex’s core number is the largest k such that it lies in a subgraph where every vertex has at","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"least k neighbors.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CoreNumbers","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/cliques\/corenumbers"}}}